    symptoms: 'Door scrapes the top corner, the latch side, or drags at the bottom; worse in humid summer weather.',
    quickCheck: ['Close the door slowly and watch the gap (the "reveal") around it. Even gap all around = good; a gap that pinches to nothing shows where it rubs.', 'Rubbing at the top latch-side corner almost always means the door has sagged on loose top hinge screws.', 'Rub chalk or a pencil on the door edge, close it a few times, and look for the transfer mark on the frame.'],
    steps: [
      {title: 'Tighten every hinge screw', detail: 'Tighten all hinge screws on the door and the frame. Do not plane anything until the hinges are tight — loose hinges are the most common cause.'},
      {title: 'Pull the door back up with one long screw', detail: 'If it still rubs at the top latch corner, replace one screw in the TOP hinge on the frame side (the one closest to the stop) with a 3-inch screw. It bites into the wall stud and pulls the jamb and door back toward the hinge side.'},
      {title: 'Fix any stripped screw holes', detail: 'If a screw spins without tightening, repair the hole first (see the stripped-screw-hole guide), then re-drive it.'},
      {title: 'Check again before removing wood', detail: 'Close the door and check the reveal. Most doors are fixed by now. If the frame or door is truly swollen, mark the tight area with a pencil.'},
      {title: 'Plane or sand only what is needed', detail: 'Remove the minimum: plane with long strokes toward the corners, or sand with 80-grit on a block. Take a little, test, repeat.'},
      {title: 'Seal the bare wood', detail: 'Prime and paint (or seal) any bare edge — top and bottom edges too if you trimmed them — so the door does not soak up moisture and swell again.'},
    ],
    tricks: ['Tom Silva of This Old House: take off the minimum when planing a swollen door, because it shrinks again in winter. Do sticking-door work in the driest part of the year when you can.', 'Aim for a reveal of about 1/8 inch (roughly the thickness of a nickel).', 'A thin bead of paste wax or a candle rubbed on a lightly rubbing edge can buy time on a seasonal swell.', 'Plane the hinge-side edge when possible — it keeps the latch-side bevel and hardware untouched.'],
    tools: ['#2 and #3 Phillips screwdriver', 'Drill/driver', 'Pencil or chalk', 'Block plane or sanding block (80/120 grit)', 'Level'],
